# Confidential OIDC BFF sessions

Run `ddd recipe oidc-bff-session --path <app> --dry-run` before applying the same
command without `--dry-run`. Generated files live in `src/shared/oidc-bff`.
Generation preflights every target, rejects symlinks/different owner content,
preserves identical reruns and rolls back only unchanged files it created.
No module, route, cookie, environment variable, business policy, package manifest,
lockfile or provider configuration is changed automatically.

Consumer runtime requirements: Node 20+ or Bun, `jose@6.2.12` and
`@redis/client@6.3.0`. Add these through the app's approved dependency workflow;
on the local Bun host use `bun add --exact jose@6.2.12 @redis/client@6.3.0`.
Preserve an existing CI/container package-manager contract and its lockfile.

`createOidcBffClient` takes immutable explicit issuer, client ID/secret, exact
HTTPS callback, configured scopes, five same-origin HTTPS metadata endpoints
(discovery, authorization, token, revocation, JWKS), and optional configured
authorization parameters. Reserved OIDC/PKCE/state parameters cannot be
overridden. The consumer owns any provider-specific audience parameter and
exact issuer/path policy. Only `client_secret_basic`, code/S256 and RS256 signed
ID tokens are supported. Required scopes include `openid` and `offline_access`;
authenticated output requires a complete access/refresh token pair.

Supply the existing server-owned pending record to `beginAuthorization` and
`exchangeAuthorizationCode`; the client does not create a second pending record.
Fresh authentication is required. It verifies signed issuer, subject, client
audience, authorized party, nonce, expiration, issued time and authentication
time after pending start, with bounded clock tolerance. Optional signed
access/code hashes must match. Requested prompt alone does not prove fresh
authentication; the provider integration must actually perform it.

Discovery must match every configured endpoint exactly. No redirect, retry,
fallback or positive-validation cache exists. A total operation deadline covers
discovery, token exchange, token custody hook, streamed JWKS, verification and
best-effort rejected-token cleanup. Responses are capped at 64 KiB; safe token
fields at 16 KiB; public RSA JWKS at 16 keys and bounded moduli. Errors have fixed
messages and never include provider bodies, credentials or raw exceptions.

The async `onTokenPairIssued` hook stages every independently bounded safe
credential before scope/type/expiry/ID-token admission policy, including
access-only or refresh-only responses. At least one safe credential is needed.
Malformed expiry uses `maxAccessTokenSeconds` only as a retirement hint; this
fallback is never admitted as a verified token set. Persist the retirement
record before proceeding. Persistence failure rejects sign-in and attempts
immediate revocation. A wholly malformed/oversize response cannot safely
disclose token material; it is rejected without session admission.

`OpaqueSessionStore` accepts injected `SessionCommands`, a trusted stable client
namespace, optional clock (integer seconds), immutable duration settings and a
**mandatory fifth argument** `OpaqueSessionNamespace` containing `restoreEpoch`.
The epoch must be a canonical lowercase UUIDv4 held outside the Redis persistence
and backup failure domain. There is no default, generated operator value or
legacy namespace fallback. Missing/invalid configuration rejects construction
before any storage command. Existing callers must pass their explicit trusted
epoch after the clock/settings arguments (use `undefined` and `{}` when omitted).
For example, the application-owned wrapper supplies its already validated
external setting:

```ts
new OpaqueSessionStore(commands, clientId, undefined, settings, {
  restoreEpoch: externallyCustodiedRestoreEpoch,
});
```

The constructor snapshots the epoch into a hash. Active browser, pending,
claim, current-session and session keys include that hash within the client's
existing Redis Cluster slot. Pending/session records also carry hash provenance.
Retirement rows, index, leases and non-admissible markers remain client-only;
rotation does not hide restored queued credentials. `RevocationClaim.epoch` is
hash provenance, not an operator value or permission. The lease itself binds
that provenance, so changing the returned claim cannot settle an old-epoch
lease through a new-epoch owner.
Browser cookies contain only random handles; pending PKCE and token material
remain server-side. Client-hashed Redis Cluster slots isolate keys. Lua owns
one-time pending claim/completion, browser/generation/client binding, fresh
account switch, current-session index, logout, staged exact-pair transfer and
durable revocation enqueue/lease/ack/retry. Only successful exact pending and
pair commit removes the staged ticket. Once any worker claims retirement, a
durable non-admissible marker prevents later session commit through retries or
expired leases; only completed retirement removes that marker. Expired access retains refresh custody.
Restart requires the same backend/client namespace and external epoch; there is no local fallback.

Defaults: session 3300s, pending 600s, browser generation 1 year, provider access
maximum 3600s, provider refresh maximum 86400s, token custody 90000s, lease 30s,
retry 60s. These are conservative documented defaults, not discovery facts.
The owner must configure provider lifetime bounds explicitly and keep custody
strictly longer than both provider maxima. Options are bounded and cannot
reduce custody below those bounds. Retirement expiry hints are not session
admission. Do not expire the whole token row with browser access expiry.

`createValkeySessionCommands` takes an explicit trusted URL, timeout and optional
test connection seam. TLS (`rediss`) is default; plaintext requires an explicit
trusted-internal opt-in. Connect and command share a bounded deadline, disable
offline queues/reconnects, and share one connection attempt. No command runs
after its connection deadline. `close` permanently retires this command owner.
No URL/token logging occurs. The Redis library owns RESP framing; this adapter
bounds deadlines/queued commands and request arguments, and the store validates
returned records. It does not claim streamed HTTP-style response byte control.

Each app still owns configuration, cookie flags, same-origin guards, Next
server-only marker, authorization wrappers, locale/navigation and durable worker
scheduling. Each request must perform current provider and canonical domain
admission. Provider traits, JWT subject or an existing browser session are not
business permissions. Per-app token retirement leaves shared provider SSO;
global logout requires its explicitly owned provider operation.

Run generated unit cases with `bun test src/shared/oidc-bff`. Actual Lua proof
additionally requires `OIDC_BFF_TEST_VALKEY_URL` pointing to a disposable Redis
database with a separately authorized profile. Tests use random client
namespaces and never flush, stop or alter the server. Unit/source evidence is
separate from CI, deployed provider behavior, browser and authorized actor proof.

Storage custody: key hashing and Redis Cluster hash tags do not encrypt the JSON
values. Access/refresh credentials and PKCE material are plaintext within the
server store. Production activation requires authenticated app-scoped Redis
ACLs, verified private/TLS transport, and verified encryption/access controls
for persistent storage and backups. An unauthenticated shared Redis instance is
not a suitable production deployment. This recipe does not provision or prove
those operator controls.


## Restore and retirement acceptance

A normal independent application logout rotates only that browser's generation.
Changing the external restore epoch is an operator recovery boundary, not an
ordinary logout or shared-provider SSO logout. Current provider/canonical checks
remain required; they cannot prove that a restored local logout generation was
not rolled back.

Before restoring Redis persistence, hold every affected application and its
retirement workers. The operator must advance each affected client's externally
custodied UUIDv4 outside the restored dataset and confirm the value cannot be
restored from the same backup. Reusing a previous epoch permits pre-logout
session/callback replay. Keep the held state until recovery reconciliation and
storage controls are verified, then start every owner with the same current
per-client epoch. A sibling client's epoch and keys remain independent.

Under the advanced epoch, old cookies, pending callbacks and already claimed
callbacks cannot resume admission; consumers must take the current pending
record before exchanging a provider code. Same-epoch process/connection restart
still works. This is explicit operator fencing, not automatic rollback detection:
a process left running with the old epoch can still address old active keys.

Retirement custody from the restored backup remains visible to the new owner.
Existing leases expire normally; old-epoch acknowledgements through a new owner
are refused and marker fences remain. A current owner can then lease and settle
retirement after the provider operation succeeds. Restored active token rows in
an abandoned epoch are not automatically enqueued or swept by this recipe.
The operator must explicitly reconcile them before service resumes.

Epoch rotation does **not** revoke provider tokens and cannot reconstruct token
material minted, queued or deleted after the backup snapshot. A stale snapshot
can lose those credentials and retirement tickets altogether. Recovery therefore
also requires an independently proven provider cleanup/revocation or conservative
provider-lifetime cutoff and externally held custody reconciliation. Never call
browser denial a successful provider retirement. This recipe creates no operator
epoch, secret, worker schedule or recovery-provider call.

The opt-in disposable Redis suite uses native `DUMP`/`RESTORE` on only random
synthetic client keys to reproduce stale snapshot rollback. It demonstrates the
unsafe reused-epoch control, rotated-epoch denial before an exchange seam,
client isolation, same-epoch restart, cross-epoch retirement visibility and
stale lease acknowledgement refusal. It does not prove deployed AOF recovery,
external custody, operator quiescence, provider cleanup or authorized actors.
